  "query_preview": "..\n\nAfter a cloudy start to the day, partly to mostly sunny skies have \nemerged across most of SE Michigan as we transition out of a deep \ntroughing pattern. The 12z KDTX RAOB sampled an h8 temperature of \n3.4 C, likely the coldest reading we will see through the work week \nwith steady warm advection already ongoing in the southwest flow \nregime. Geopotential heights are starting to build as well, but will \nbe briefly disrupted tonight as a northern stream PV anomaly shears \nout across the Great Lakes. Minimal impact from this wave though as \nit interacts with the dry airmass in place (PWAT of 0.30\"), equating \nto just an uptick in cloud cover tonight. Weakening inversion and \nthe overall dry airmass limits fog potential overnight to just \nlocalized/patchy occurrence if at all. \n\nColumn stability strengthens substantially through Thursday as a 586 \ndam ridge (500mb) builds firmly into the Ohio Valley, leading to dry \nweather. This is paired with a 1035 mb surface high drifting toward \nthe mid-Atlantic, placing SE Michigan within an extended period of \nsouthwest flow and gradual warm advection. As airmass modification \ntakes place, expecting a gradual climb in temperatures each day with \nhighs approaching 60 degrees by the end of the work week. A more \nactive upper level pattern develops during this timeframe as a \nseries of shortwaves attempt to break down the well-established \nridge. The first of these waves will track across Ontario Thursday. \nWhile moisture transport is directed well north of the Great Lakes \nwith this system, the low level jet will clip the cwa to support a \nbreezy day Thursday with gusts of 25-30 mph possible. The \ndeformation and baroclinic axis left in the wake of this system \nserve as a second focus for cyclogenesis Friday, supporting our next \nopportunity for precipitation over the weekend.",
